    title: "Fergie Chambers released in Spain as US admits court translation errors",
    summary: "Fergie Chambers released in Spain as US admits court translation errors Submitted by Amandla Thomas-Johnson on Fri, 11/20/2026 - 15:53 American pro-Palestinian activist is 'happy to breathe fresh air' following conditional release from prison as he fights US extradition request Fergie Chambers outside Aranjuez prison following his release on Friday, 20 November 2026 (Reuters) Off Fergie Chambers, the American pro-Palestinian donor detained in Spain following a US extradition request, was granted provisional release on Friday after the Trump administration admitted to “substantial” translation errors in court documents submitted in support of its case. Speaking outside Aranjuez prison on Friday following his release, Chambers described the charges against him as “baseless” and said he hoped his release was “the first step in a process of recognising that internationally”. “I think this case is important for human rights, for people who want to speak out anywhere in the world,” he said. The decision by the Criminal Chamber of Spain's National Court, known as the Audiencia Nacional, comes after Chambers had spent more than four months in custody since his arrest in Ibiza in July. In its ruling, seen by Middle East Eye, the court said errors in the translation of documents submitted by US authorities had cast doubt on the reliability of the extradition request and weakened its basis. The judges found that the development appeared to reduce the risk of Chambers fleeing and meant that his continued detention could no longer be justified. His release is subject to several conditions, including weekly appearances before the court nearest his residence every Monday, surrendering his passport, a prohibition on leaving Spain and an obligation to appear before the court whenever summoned. The court has also suspended its decision on whether to grant extradition while the translation issues are examined. 'I think this case is important for human rights, for people who want to speak out anywhere in the world' - Fergie Chambers, activist According to Friday's ruling, US authorities acknowledged in supplementary information submitted to the court on 13 November that the Spanish translation of a sworn statement by an FBI special agent contained errors described as “substantial and involuntary”. The US Department of Justice said the translation had been prepared by a privately contracted company. Chambers' lawyers, however, have challenged that explanation. In a submission to the court on 17 November, his defence argued that the errors suggested US authorities had acted in bad faith by deliberately altering the account of the facts underpinning the extradition request to make the allegations against Chambers appear more serious. The defence requested that an expert report examining the translations be admitted into evidence, that the expert be allowed to testify and answer questions and that a new official translation be prepared by a court-appointed sworn translator. The court referred these requests to prosecutors for a response and suspended its decision on the extradition request pending further consideration. Irish home raided Chambers appeared in court last Friday, describing Israel's war on Gaza as “the prevailing moral question of our time”. His release also follows a reported raid by Irish police on his Dublin home on Thursday, seizing electronic devices and clothing, reportedly at the request of US authorities. Irish police raid Palestine donor Fergie Chambers’ Dublin home at behest of Trump sperate fishing expedition”. Speaking after Chambers' release, Schnabel welcomed the decision but warned that his legal ordeal was far from over. “We will not stop fighting until Fergie is free from the threat of extradition,” she said. “We still have a cloud of uncertainty hanging over us,” she added, warning that Chambers could face up to 30 years in prison if extradited to the US and convicted. “Our baby is due in March, and we have no idea whether Fergie will be by my side when our baby is born or whether he will be in a US supermax prison.” Outside the prison, Chambers described the emotional toll of his detention, during which he said he had been separated from his family and left uncertain about his future. “These four months have felt like 40 years because everything has been so unknown and because I missed my children and my wife,” he said. “In the context of just being alone in a cell all the time, it feels like a long time. I really missed my family and friends.” Chambers said his mother had been visiting him in prison when guards informed him of the decision to release him. UK: Activist scales Spanish embassy in London in protest over Fergie Chambers case nocked on my door and said, ‘Give us five minutes, we have some good news for you.’” He described the news as a “sweet moment”, adding that he would now be able to spend time with his mother during her visit to Spain. Chambers also thanked supporters who had written to him during his detention. “I got letters from all over the place these past months. People who don't know me across Spain,” he said, expressing gratitude to those who had shown solidarity and taken “a stand for justice”. Despite the ordeal, Chambers said his detention had to be understood in the context of the wider Palestinian struggle. “At the end of the day, I know that the sacrifice I've had to make is very small in relation to the struggle that we are all a part of for the Palestinian people, for their liberation, sovereignty and respect, for their right to live.” For now, he said, he was simply relieved to be outside. “Thank God for today. I'm just happy to breathe some fresh air.” Human Rights News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
