    id: "bb98b992ff44e74ba9b8",
    cat: "ai",
    categories: ["ai"],
    title: "Anthropic shares fall after blockbuster opening as wider tech sell-off continues",
    summary: "Other tech companies have also taken a hit as share markets broadly slump.",
    preview: "Anthropic's shares have dropped back towards their initial price after a soaring first day on the stock market, as a wider tech sell-off deepens. The fall in tech stocks continued to weigh on markets through Friday, with the S&P 500 down about 2.8% and the Dow Jones Industrial Average down more than 1% by mid-afternoon. Both US indexes touched correction territory, a point where they are trading at 10% or more down from a recent high.",
    body: "Anthropic's shares have dropped back towards their initial price after a soaring first day on the stock market, as a wider tech sell-off deepens.\n\nThe fall in tech stocks continued to weigh on markets through Friday, with the S&P 500 down about 2.8% and the Dow Jones Industrial Average down more than 1% by mid-afternoon.\n\nBoth US indexes touched correction territory, a point where they are trading at 10% or more down from a recent high.\n\nThe tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index also fell more than 4% by afternoon trading as shares in major tech companies continued to fall.\n\nThe speed at which AI investment has been growing - with more than $1 trillion spent in the last year alone by a small handful of major tech companies - has led to ongoing concerns of a financial bubble. Questions are being raised over whether those investments will pay off, and when.\n\nDespite a positive first day of trading, Anthropic's shares fell on Friday, dropping at one point by more than 12% to about $85, close to its initial public offering (IPO) price of $83.\n\nThe artificial intelligence (AI) company's introduction to the Nasdaq stock exchange on Thursday was a crucial test for the industry and was largely seen as a success, with trading starting at $111.50.\n\nThe maker of the Claude chatbot raised a record $81bn in its initial public offering (IPO), but its share price moved significantly in the first day of trading.\n\nThe shares peaked at $115 on Thursday, before easing to end the day up 17% at $97.17.\n\nAnalysts said volatility was to be expected in the first days or even weeks of Anthropic's trading, as the company was the first frontier AI firm to go public so had no direct peers.\n\nSome have also noted the similarities with Elon Musk's SpaceX, which at the time of its debut in June was the largest IPO on record. Shares in the rocket and AI company experienced large swings in its first few weeks of trading.\n\nMost tech companies see their shares fall after hitting the stock market, often after reporting quarterly results for the first time.\n\nBut many, including Meta, Amazon and Google, have gone on to become the largest and most profitable corporations in the world.\n\nEd Moya, a strategist at market intelligence firm AlphaSense, said Anthropic's blockbuster IPO did not appear to have restored confidence in the broader AI market.\n\nWhile AI demand was still very strong, Moya told the BBC that investors were trying to answer the question of how much were they willing to pay for that growth.\n\n\"As we've gone through the past past few weeks, it seems that there's been more risks that have surfaced,\" he said.\n\n\"Right now you're starting to see Wall Street is trying to shift from rewarding growth at any price, to demanding stronger cash flow and a clearer path to profitability.\"\n\nShares in some of the largest US tech companies also fell on Friday as the market sell off continued.\n\nNvidia shares had plunged by more than 11% by the afternoon, while Musk's firms SpaceX and Tesla had both fallen by more than 6%.\n\nMeta was down more than 5% and Amazon was down more than 3.5%, while Apple and Alphabet experienced smaller falls.\n\nEurope's main stock markets - which contain fewer tech stocks - also took a hit.\n\nGermany's Dax index closed down 2.6%, France's CAC 40 was down 1.9% and the UK's FTSE 100 ended trading 1% lower.\n\nEarlier on Friday, tech-heavy Asian markets saw steep falls.\n\nSouth Korea's Kospi and Japan's Nikkei 225 indexes closed down about 5%, with shares in memory chip makers Sk Hynix, Kioxia and Tokyo Electron, and other tech firms such as Samsung Electronics, seeing losses.\n\nTaiwan's stock market dropped about 6% on Friday, taking the weekly fall to more than 17% and what was reported to be its biggest weekly fall since 1990.",
